# Fleet packet contract — Cursor keystone lane (fleet-ops#1545)

This lane works under the same three contracts the Pi lanes carry. The contract
text below REUSES the Pi packet format and verdict-line grammar verbatim; do
not rewrite either into a cursor-specific dialect.

## 1. Packet in — work enters as a sealed packet, never an ad-hoc prompt

- Take work only as a sealed packet from the queue, with claim discipline
  identical to the pi-issue lane:
  - claim the issue first: `claim/issue-<N>` branch, work only inside the
    claimed worktree,
  - never start from a bare "do this please" prompt that is not a packet,
  - if work is not a packet, name the gap to Nish instead of improvising.
- A packet run must carry its issue identity (`Nishfleet/<repo>#<N>` or
  `claim/issue-<N>`) in the first user turn so the run is attributable.

## 2. Verdict out — every run ends with a machine-checkable verdict line

End EVERY run with a verdict line, same grammar as the Pi lanes (this is the
line the pi packet-verdict extension and guard_pi_packet.py check):

```
PACKET-VERDICT tools=<n> class=<worked|no-tools>
```

- `tools=<n>` = number of tool calls made this run;
- `class=worked` if any tool ran, `class=no-tools` if the run was text-only.
- The line must be the LAST assistant text of the run. A run that ends without
  a verdict line is an empty run — do not report it as dispatched/working.

The file `lib/guard_cursor_packet.py` (fleet-ops) runs the same
`guard_pi_packet` verdict-line grammar over cursor agent-transcripts; treat a
`guard-cursor-packet:` warning on your transcript as a flagged empty run.

## 3. Delivery evidence at close

- Close means a PR (or commit with evidence), same as the Pi lanes: the close
  must be earned by a merge, not by a comment.
- Output flows through the identical merge gates as every other lane.
- No agent attribution in commits, the PR, or comments.

